Canonical Source Boundary
===============================================================================

Status: source boundaries contract.

This document defines the source ownership boundary for the syntax
reimplementation after declared operation contracts-the managed memory runtime. `docs/design/specification.mec` is the sole
language authority. The current Nom entry point is inventoried only so the
atomic cutover can remove every caller.

The authoritative inventories are:

- `grammar-audit/recursive-core.tsv`: the exact 80-rule component; and
- `grammar-audit/recursive-core-syntax-schema.tsv`: the rule-level canonical tree
  contract.

1. Ownership
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The syntax crate owns UTF-8 source revisions, scanning, lossless trees, parser
recovery, structural diagnostics, strict structural validation, typed red-tree
views, and incremental identity.

Source tools consume canonical syntax directly:

```text
formatter / HTML renderer   DocumentSyntax plus lossless tokens
source index / resolver     typed imports, exports, contexts, scopes, paths
CLI source classification   typed document and Mech item structure
editor / interactive input  SyntaxSnapshot, revisions, diagnostics
```

The engine owns source semantics. Its canonical source frontend consumes a
strictly valid typed document and constructs the semantic forms used by
`SourceProgram`, `ProgramArtifact`, Type System v1, and memory planning.

The runtime owns activation, state, scheduling, resources, effects, and host
interfaces. It does not interpret concrete syntax.

2. Prohibited boundaries
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Canonical syntax, validation, and source-semantic modules do not call the Nom
parser or compare their output to it. The syntax crate does not depend on the
engine or runtime and does not construct compiler IR, `SourceProgram`,
`ProgramArtifact`, `Value`, memory plans, or runtime cells.

There is no canonical-to-`mech_core::Program` lowering tree, parser selector,
fallback route, or compatibility adapter. Source tools use the canonical document root; execution uses the engine source
frontend.

3. Strict validation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Parsing is total and lossless. Strict validation is the gate for consumers that
require a complete source program. A snapshot fails strict validation when it
contains any of:

- parser diagnostics with error severity;
- an `ERROR` node or token;
- a `MISSING` node or token;
- a required typed child or token that is absent;
- token text invalid for its canonical syntax kind; or
- a parser resource-limit diagnostic that leaves the requested root incomplete.

Validation diagnostics use these stable fields:

```text
code       validation/<stable-condition>
phase      SyntaxValidation
severity   Error
rule       canonical RuleId when one owns the condition
context    internal ParserContextId when recovery owns the condition
primary    structural anchor when possible, absolute revision range otherwise
expected   canonical syntax kinds or terminals when applicable
found      observed canonical kind or token when applicable
recovery   ResourceLimit for exhausted parser resources
tags       stable diagnostic classification flags when applicable
```

English messages are presentation. Codes, phases, rules, anchors, expected and
found syntax, and tags are the test contract.

Validation never constructs partial semantic values. Consumers that can work
with incomplete input use the snapshot and typed optional accessors directly;
they do not invoke strict source semantics.

4. Canonical document fixtures
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The shared source fixtures in tests/fixtures/syntax-source-boundary cover empty
input, executable source, mixed documents, source indexing, interactive mutation,
compiler structures, configuration, browser documents, and malformed input.
canonical_document_root executes their specification-derived accept/reject
expectations and checks lossless source reconstruction. Cargo module boundaries
and isolated package builds establish the supported public source entry points.

5. Semantic ownership and artifact completion
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The canonical source producer and resident providers share maintained operation
contracts from mech-core. Producer feature selection does not change variadic
construction, shape, alias, or change-detection policies. A provider-independent
contract describes semantics; availability of a resident implementation remains
an activation concern.

An undeclared source input may acquire a concrete schema from expression-local
peer constraints before a SourceProgram is finalized. Unannotated local pattern
projections from unresolved inputs can likewise acquire constrained element kinds;
their unresolved generator retains the existing source/bind artifact boundary.
An explicitly Dynamic
input is a boxed runtime value and cannot escape through a fabricated conversion:
Type System v1 has no cast from Dynamic to a concrete scalar or aggregate.
Optional construction preserves existing option layers and live dependencies.
A present Dynamic option boxes the original typed snapshot, including its schema
and shape, through the canonical ValueDraft constructor.

Constant-endpoint ranges activate and produce their specified values. Ranges
with live input or state endpoints retain the existing resident completion gate:
fixed arenas cannot support changing range cardinality. A correct peer-inferred
SourceProgram and serializable artifact do not claim that runtime capability;
activation rejects live ranges even when initial shape facts are supplied.

Selection preserves the source element kind and sequential subscript order.
Record and table fields use canonical Id selectors. Boolean selector populations
are bounded turn dimensions; activation supplies their resolved cardinality.
Existing resident arenas remain fixed for an activation, and a later mask with a
different selected cardinality must fail before publication. Set construction
retains the canonical duplicate-key rejection policy.

SourceProgram input names and source anchors remain canonical source identities.
At compile_artifact, every external input name uses the existing hexadecimal
interface-transport mechanism under the mech-source-input- namespace. The public
artifact_input_name, encode_source_input_name, and decode_source_input_name
accessors expose the injective, reversible transport mapping. This permits paths
and Unicode names without changing source identity or artifact ABI. Transporting
@ctx/path as an input does not implement context or capability resolution.

Source-only constructs without an executable operation contract retain an
explicit artifact completion boundary. Publishing an intermediate semantic graph
is not evidence that an artifact activates. The source/runtime regressions distinguish these
boundaries from supported selection, construction, and matrix operations whose
actual output values and changing input turns are tested.

Computed mutable initialization is artifact-owned: SourceState retains its initial
SourceValue, which becomes a constant or activation-slot initializer. The resident
activation graph computes pure values once and copies them into managed state.
Arithmetic, matrices, and earlier computed immutable bindings survive bytecode
roundtrip. A live initializer is complete artifact semantics but receives a precise
resident capability result before activation. No deferred initializer sidecar or
frontend evaluator participates.

Named call bindings use parameter metadata on the shared function type declaration.
Maintained binary arithmetic declares left/right; names reorder semantic operands
before overload selection. Unknown, duplicate, missing, and excess bindings fail
with source anchors. Functions without name metadata require positional arguments.
